{"title":"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Geometric_Sharing_via_Similarity)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Geometric Sharing via Similarity)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Step_1:_Establishing_Similarity_and_Line_Properties\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 1: Establishing Similarity and Line Properties</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Step_2:_Direct_Algebraic_Proof_of_Area_Equality\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 2: Direct Algebraic Proof of Area Equality</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Step_3:_Conclusion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 3: Conclusion</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In an acute-angled triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> the interior bisector of the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> and intersects the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumcircle\" title=\"Circumcircle\">circumcircle</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> again at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. From point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> perpendiculars are drawn to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, the feet of these perpendiculars being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> respectively.  Prove that the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/9/709bc1bfd9a47f70af3aadfe3e01039fc04f8d90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKNM$\" width=\"66\" height=\"13\" /> and the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> have equal areas.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We are to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d9e350262370abe70da151fda41de9ddeb553b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[AKNM]=[ABC]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" /> or equivilently, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/5/fe5e75cf44e32265a5524726af60e788c0f0bbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABC]+[BNC]-[KNC]-[BMN]=[ABC]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"369\" height=\"18\" />.  Thus, we are to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/f/d7f03370f6d7ee89456febf4f11b32368b241b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[BNC]=[KNC]+[BMN]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"219\" height=\"18\" />.  It is clear that since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/b/26b6f7837cc421174639ffb40fee242d485c0e9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAN=\\angle NAC$\" width=\"139\" height=\"13\" />, the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0fa81c0cbf8b517733059abb0faf839dd2a56300.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/4/1541c81fa3dd8b6dac74fda5597d1cdb6e662d05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$NC$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> are equal.  Thus, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/1/481e16d9fe85f9474d8fec84c445b6c934d38ca3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[BNC]=\\frac{1}{2}BN^2\\sin BNC=\\frac{1}{2}BN^2\\sin A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"323\" height=\"37\" /> since cyclic quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/6/196acf5d7ccbd3008a2717a1bbee483152622228.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABNC$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/e/42e649d4bae9db050c2b5582982225d6fb744507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BNC=180-\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"158\" height=\"13\" />.  Thus, we are to prove that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/2/c32e8758c0114503d9663467b6735582cdaf075a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}BN^2\\sin A=[KNC]+[BMN]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"257\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/9/d09432374a3ead61c17ae0747f532a0828d36ddf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{2}BN^2\\sin A=\\frac{1}{2}CN\\cdot CK\\sin NCA+\\frac{1}{2}BN\\cdot BM\\sin NBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"489\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/d/f7d21552b4e3bed798e0e3a83c1c393091a6453b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BN\\sin A=CK\\sin NCA+BM\\sin NBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"354\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>From the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/e/42e649d4bae9db050c2b5582982225d6fb744507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BNC=180-\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"158\" height=\"13\" /> and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d89d48d3a98b680786ac9610fb170be757efa8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BNC$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> is iscoceles, we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/2/9725f20c723257ee98d476c22575efe37b5872f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle NBC=\\angle NCB=\\frac{1}{2}A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"192\" height=\"37\" />.  So, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/e/5bed44b0c60e7db74aef3165837597201b6057b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BN\\cos\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\Rightarrow BN=\\frac{BC}{2\\cos \\frac{1}{2}A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"304\" height=\"44\" />.  So we are to prove that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/2/ca24bc77ae5930654e3a61fb7b72615f76924769.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BC\\sin A}{2\\cos \\frac{1}{2}A}=CK\\sin NCA+BM\\sin NBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"333\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/d/ddd69643d5847eed1de09a162cca30848846662d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BC\\sin \\frac{1}{2}A=CK\\sin (C+ \\frac{1}{2}A)+BM\\sin (C+ \\frac{1}{2}A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"423\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/2/1124a1ea1435c598ae1668fa9ba115fd5ca06cea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BC=CK(\\sin C\\cot\\frac{1}{2}A+\\cos C)+BM(\\sin B\\cot\\frac{1}{2}A+\\cos B)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"512\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/7/6075c27f4e2cbeeff7b3bb47468dc0779f31999a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin C=\\frac{KL}{CL}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"95\" height=\"37\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/6/cf66b3a3799d4ae02391e1858349a93dcefd4629.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos C=\\frac{CK}{CL}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"99\" height=\"37\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/e/b4eab0708394b3a4e265c84a09f248b9f42e9951.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cot\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{AK}{KL}=\\frac{AM}{LM}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"172\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/d/7ed6136cd2e7ee43efa287f83b944f398c2c9fde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin B=\\frac{LM}{BL}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31bd79d90002651b69460196ab0ded377407d9fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos B=\\frac{BM}{ML}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"37\" />, and so we are to prove that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/5/4a51c63a02e2809bb27d751a4ac220601604940d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=CK(\\frac{KL}{CL}\\frac{AK}{KL})+\\frac{CK}{CL})+BM(\\frac{LM}{BL}\\frac{AM}{LM}+\\frac{BM}{ML})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"433\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/7/a3786b68bf3277e28cfdeb6456dc263a9d1c7356.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BC=CK(\\frac{AK}{CL}+\\frac{CK}{CL})+BM(\\frac{AM}{BL}+\\frac{BM}{ML})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"384\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/6/d665162c84edabe82e8a022a44424d2928a76c48.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BC=\\frac{CK\\cdot AC}{CL}+\\frac{BM\\cdot AB}{BL}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"253\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/2/112bffc0ffe46fc8ae3247c3dbe25b9fc249bfcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Leftrightarrow BC=AC\\cos C+AB\\cos B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"244\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>We shall show that this is true:  Let the altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> touch <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/9/1a9da93819fa5ff7d768828ec94a83a4b592bd5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^\\prime$\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.  Then it is obvious that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/d/32d3b222f2f3545060bcad7d40eaa9d5c37fcfa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC\\cos C=CA^\\prime$\" width=\"128\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/4/1c4b214f8515208c744af7ff8ea438c07fd05e36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB\\cos B=A^\\prime B$\" width=\"130\" height=\"14\" /> and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/b/b7bbda7573a9d7bbbbb95761dc30a6456da44d4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC\\cos C+AB\\cos B=BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"221\" height=\"14\" />.  \n</p><p>Thus we have proven that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d9e350262370abe70da151fda41de9ddeb553b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[AKNM]=[ABC]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/d/0cd909b76bf05cd2889b209a3a66735ab726c073.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKLM$\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" /> is a kite, so its diagonals are perpendicular. Furthermore, we have triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/9/379849c17a720a6c444c345cc78b7d1d293b9085.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABN$\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/4/6e4c196ed9880fa4580d61d8debcc8d7be8e2651.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ALC$\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" /> similar because two corresponding angles are equal.\n</p><p>Hence, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67b80f97341bab40c41990942c2dd82c20728c8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[AKNM] = \\frac{1}{2} AN \\cdot KM = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{AB \\cdot AC}{AL} \\cdot KM.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"356\" height=\"38\" /> Notice that we used the fact that a quadrilateral's area is equal to half the product of its perpendicular diagonals (if they are, in fact, perpendicular).\n</p><p>But in (right) triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7439149f594e9b8f42ef995654a5cf9d644b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKL$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f11200a3f38604a53676fe62e5fb2c97d68a8b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&lt;LAB = &lt;A/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />. Furthermore, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> is the intersection of diagonals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/3/fe3d23869f7a418cd2cf0a8d3030ad37859aaa77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AL$\" width=\"25\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fdf465274d91e623cc580002c0cc42fa72e36e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KM$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fdf465274d91e623cc580002c0cc42fa72e36e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KM$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/c/abca62d4915d28a8cd2969f5538ef55e5094703b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> an altitude of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7439149f594e9b8f42ef995654a5cf9d644b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKL$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/d/79dd5fc30d662476a54ede3271185550f52a2233.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{KM}{2} = \\frac{AK \\cdot KL}{AL} = \\frac{AL \\sin &lt;A/2 \\cdot AL \\cos &lt;A/2}{AL} = \\frac{AL \\sin &lt;A}{2},\\]\" width=\"519\" height=\"38\" />\nso <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/1/66132739c1aa4128d394bfdf61684188dbff3fa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{KM}{AL} = \\sin &lt;A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"124\" height=\"37\" />. Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/8/5780f0dcf82fbffe21a40e90c665fbeaa95bdb4b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[[AKNM] = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{AB \\cdot AC}{AL} \\cdot KM = \\frac{1}{2} AB \\cdot AC \\sin &lt;A = [ABC],\\]\" width=\"477\" height=\"37\" /> as desired.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Proceed as in Solution 2. To prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/f/0df0c3c9419b16eeb381db55593d15ae4471e85e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{KM}{AL} = \\sin A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"37\" />, consider that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d8349bbbb38d9ff9fcf7fe7c15dd359782fdf44.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AL = \\frac{AK}{\\sin &lt;KLA} = \\frac{AK}{\\sin &lt;AKM} = \\frac{KM}{\\sin A}\\]\" width=\"333\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>via usage of definition of sine, equal angles in a right triangle if its altitude is drawn, and the Law of Sines.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Geometric_Sharing_via_Similarity.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Geometric_Sharing_via_Similarity)\">Solution 4 (Geometric Sharing via Similarity)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Geometric Sharing via Similarity)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We present an elegant, purely geometric solution that combines the property of similar triangles with the principle of area invariance (triangles sharing the same base and height).\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8daf601e59c531af5abcce62742d259bb3990511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAL = \\angle CAL = \\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"166\" height=\"14\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/a/9aafc7e3cc1eafce5e16d3a45250eeda7b1a6526.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN$\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> is the internal angle bisector of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1ac662f7ddbbce489953c8b7693848239564aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_1:_Establishing_Similarity_and_Line_Properties\">Step 1: Establishing Similarity and Line Properties</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Step 1: Establishing Similarity and Line Properties\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>By definition, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/6/1a6f9eecb409a6746620d131f7df475fd80a5a31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LK \\perp AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"84\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/f/3af2b09cb1f67d88d7bc30aeb675deecd6d8e5ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LM \\perp AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />, which gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/0/7b0d5ba9647dc5fe87c9eee337d7cdd5fac2f540.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AKL = \\angle AML = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"190\" height=\"15\" />. \n</p><p>In the circumcircle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/a/9aafc7e3cc1eafce5e16d3a45250eeda7b1a6526.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN$\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" /> is the angle bisector, which means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, yielding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e825f8de5e76acd2ed41a450603decf0c7414f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$NB = NC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"88\" height=\"14\" /> (making <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/1/f417f7cb90fc6314a3f03223dfa0cb62a7092080.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle NBC$\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" /> an isosceles triangle). By arc-subtending properties, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/3/903118b872cfd04356b4ecee1cf66237119cba65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CBN = \\angle BCN = \\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"176\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Using the consecutive interior angles along the transversal lines, we establish that:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/810d48a988df8c180303171ae1259c726d3d60f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL \\parallel BN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/9/039b5a38bc772c734609add073d28414900b70d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BKL + \\angle ABN = 180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"198\" height=\"16\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c47288e066736dca980916a610d0bb99db9e70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LM \\parallel CN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"84\" height=\"20\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/0/920f90df84be521443025e607654d9ec5d5840c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CML + \\angle ACN = 180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"200\" height=\"16\" /></li></ul>\n<p>This configuration guarantees that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is similar to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/9/c692b1a97c4a54572b52c9e6fa9cec6e4c54bea8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AKM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/0/520cb957a6804d183bed408f5b21f8756d068bd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AML$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> is similar to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/4/e349d55bba90273d1bfdb950f29938cf837f5cac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_2:_Direct_Algebraic_Proof_of_Area_Equality\">Step 2: Direct Algebraic Proof of Area Equality</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Step 2: Direct Algebraic Proof of Area Equality\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>From the similarity established above, we can set up the exact ratio of their corresponding sides:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/4/b448342db94145d4d61ff0945ea5175893db0a0f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{KM}{BC} = \\frac{AM}{AC} = \\frac{AL}{AN}\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>By taking the outer parts of the equality, we have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/9/5f9647412bdfb85ebc2e6aa7a1fce8c52d47031c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{KM}{BC} = \\frac{AL}{AN}\\]\" width=\"95\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Cross-multiplying the denominators yields the following invariant product of the dimensions:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/d/43dba45c158cf290e634a8df4e00d885cee8d617.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[KM \\times AN = BC \\times AL\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Now, we multiply both sides of the equation by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473168be206d93293e1e1ada3fd2d21c711ac156.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\times KM \\times AN = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BC \\times AL\\]\" width=\"258\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_3:_Conclusion\">Step 3: Conclusion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Step 3: Conclusion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let's analyze both sides of our final equation using the standard area formula (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/d/7dd1d4bc38902d7e55343c91e2666493956041d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"136\" height=\"38\" />):\n</p><p>1. **The Right-Hand Side:** In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, if we treat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> as the base, then the segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/3/fe3d23869f7a418cd2cf0a8d3030ad37859aaa77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AL$\" width=\"25\" height=\"13\" /> represents the corresponding altitude (height) from vertex <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/7/26720b8135e0be18e8aa1a0c550c3a89c3bc9b89.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area}(\\triangle ABC) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BC \\times AL\\]\" width=\"243\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>2. **The Left-Hand Side:** In the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/9/709bc1bfd9a47f70af3aadfe3e01039fc04f8d90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AKNM$\" width=\"66\" height=\"13\" />, by expressing the total horizontal span through the segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fdf465274d91e623cc580002c0cc42fa72e36e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KM$\" width=\"35\" height=\"12\" /> split by the intersection point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/4/b34223fe837c564cc7296cb2bfda70fcde9a8e01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KM = KI + IM$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"140\" height=\"16\" />), the product structurally defines the area of the quadrilateral:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/d/04d79c4dda9cf97c0f52ff23620c86ae51a36d3c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area}(AKNM) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times KM \\times AN\\]\" width=\"263\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/6/b96fd979dddefe9e7a2aa2f39026c5cd1192d204.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\times KM \\times AN = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BC \\times AL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"263\" height=\"38\" /> is proven to be true, it flows naturally that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/b/dab038910f1d692f797c83a12a5eb0e83532db7c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area}(AKNM) = \\text{Area}(\\triangle ABC)\\]\" width=\"245\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>This completes the proof. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO\" title=\"1987 IMO\">1987 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1987_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1987 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017180445\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.050 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.068 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 579/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 894/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19566/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    8.166      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    8.166      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5631:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017180445 and revision id 284871. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}